592186045417] [17592186545767 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186545356 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545767 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186545414 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545767 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186545442 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545767 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186545603 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545767 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545767 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545767 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186545356 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545767 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "c2702c34-c7e9-5325-9741-8ce3622c97cd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545787 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186545248 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545787 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186545345 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545787 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186545352 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545787 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186545650 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545787 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186545662 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545787 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545787 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545787 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186545662 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545787 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f26454ee-b84b-57c9-bd6b-8ffaabec57a1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545794 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186545742 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545794 #edb/ref 1069 "q-134089" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545794 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545794 #edb/ref 1071 "It's known that there is a weak positive linear correlation between the variables $x$ and $y.$ Which of the following scatter plots corresponds to this situation?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545794 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "06e32974-1b71-50c8-93f5-929432644d25" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545794 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine the given situation.\n\n- The *positive* linear correlation means that the data points should be scattered along a line with a *positive* slope.\n\n- Since the correlation is *weak*, most of the points should be scattered *far* from the trend line.\n\nTherefore, this situation corresponds to the following scatter plot.\n\n![](images/8c/8c21367686723d7ce1165ec24f569658d77376a39d7962b8a4ed90b06c9e1220.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545798 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186545704 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545798 #edb/ref 1069 "q-134122" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545798 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545798 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/f2/f2f569fe07310e8bd068cda0152b296d7b1189efec03720a0d3a043a7ab52d21.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above shows the relationship between the age of some people and the average number of cups of coffee they consume daily. \nWhich of the following is the best approximation of the correlation coefficient?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545798 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "09efb2e6-1e80-53f4-bf82-55db52baf7d8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545798 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the linear correlation coefficient $\\rho $ satisfies the inequality\n\n$$\n-1\\le \\rho \\le 1,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\rho =1$ indicates a perfect positive correlation, and $\\rho =-1$ indicates a perfect negative correlation.\n\nFrom the plot, it seems that the data points are scattered randomly. Therefore, there is no linear correlation.\n\nTherefore, the best approximation of the correlation coefficient must be $\\rho ≈0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545811 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186545681 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545811 #edb/ref 1069 "q-134126" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545811 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545811 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/27/271e5f80ff6ab311bcdbc6b2725516b16a5c3fee63b6d219a59801c78d4f77e4.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above shows the relationship between the number of shirts and the number of pants sold in a store over a certain number of days. \nWhich of the following is the best approximation of the correlation coefficient?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545811 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2e20c6e9-8a1f-5520-84a5-13e0b1715760" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545811 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the linear correlation coefficient $\\rho $ satisfies the inequality\n\n$$\n-1\\le \\rho \\le 1,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\rho =1$ indicates a perfect positive correlation, and $\\rho =-1$ indicates a perfect negative correlation.\n\nFrom the plot, it seems that the data points are scattered randomly. Therefore, there is no linear correlation.\n\nTherefore, the best approximation of the correlation coefficient must be $\\rho ≈0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545841 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186545787 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545841 #edb/ref 1069 "q-134125" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545841 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545841 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/4c/4c03191b325ee430f02216f35998db128ea99c6096222d489fa817f680e98d10.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above shows the relationship between the time some college students spend studying at night for their final exams and the number of hours they sleep per night. Which of the following is the best approximation of the correlation coefficient?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545841 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9f7eb28b-e6d3-58a7-ad9a-3a6681ca1ee4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545841 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the linear correlation coefficient $\\rho $ satisfies the inequality\n\n$$\n-1\\le \\rho \\le 1,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\rho =1$ indicates a perfect positive correlation, and $\\rho =-1$ indicates a perfect negative correlation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our scatter plot.\n\n![](images/74/74aa691e42eea766595eede7d42d59e164509d6dbf3c8a609dd42e1196d11512.png)\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The points in the scatter plot seem to follow a straight line with a *negative* slope. So, the correlation coefficient must be *negative*. Thus, we disregard the following values:\n\n$$\n\\rho ≈0.8,\\,\\rho ≈0.4,\\,\\rho ≈0\n$$\n\n- The points are scattered quite far from the corresponding trend line. So, the correlation coefficient must be closer to $0.$ Thus, we disregard the following value:\n\n$$\n\\rho ≈-0.9\n$$\n\nTherefore, the best approximation of the correlation coefficient must be $\\rho ≈-0.3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545848 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186545674 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545848 #edb/ref 1069 "q-134099" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545848 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545848 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/25/2561ac832231961f439731ff16a5b918787b848fc41f77fb63e987c79d50f40b.png)\n\nA study is made on the grades of a group of high school students. The scatter plot above shows the relationship between grades obtained in the subjects of chemistry and mathematics. Which of the following best describes the correlation between the variables in the scatter plot?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545848 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "abc49f5c-860a-5246-8fd2-ba05f76f3a6e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545848 #edb/ref 1074 "From the plot, it seems that the data points are scattered randomly. Therefore, there is no linear correlation." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545854 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186545701 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545854 #edb/ref 1069 "q-134090" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545854 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545854 #edb/ref 1071 "It's known that there is a strong positive linear correlation between the variables $x$ and $y.$ Which of the following scatter plots corresponds to this situation?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545854 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bc9c7169-1131-5a87-93ac-6b79642c5534" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545854 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine the given situation.\n\n- The *positive* linear correlation means that the data points should be scattered along a line with a *positive* slope.\n\n- Since the correlation is *strong*, most of the points should be scattered *close* to the trend line.\n\nTherefore, this situation corresponds to the following scatter plot.\n\n![](images/f9/f918c0b1ec7a3b3969fef43eb60bba0b4d4afba813082d710c456d4c53a49018.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545862 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186545691 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545862 #edb/ref 1069 "q-134112" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545862 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545862 #edb/ref 1071 "It's known that there is a linear correlation between the height (in $\\text{cm}$) and weight (in $\\text{kg}$) of a group of students in a particular class. Given that the correlation coefficient is $\\rho ≈0.45,$ which of the following scatter plots corresponds to this situation?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545862 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d339220f-f17c-55cf-8865-ba521fdebae7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545862 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the linear correlation coefficient $\\rho $ satisfies the inequality\n\n$$\n-1\\le \\rho \\le 1,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\rho =1$ indicates a perfect positive correlation, and $\\rho =-1$ indicates a perfect negative correlation.\n\nTherefore, since $\\rho ≈0.45,$ there is a *weak positive* linear correlation in the data.\n\n- The *positive* linear correlation means that the data points should be scattered along a line with a *positive* slope.\n\n- Since the correlation is *weak*, most of the points should be scattered *far* from the trend line.\n\nTherefore, this situation corresponds to the following scatter plot.\n\n![](images/31/31aef47ad76d8cd4c7e967ccc7b8ca523eb3a20af440454bc7908bbe9441c918.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545866 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186545767 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545866 #edb/ref 1069 "q-134103" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545866 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545866 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/04/04fbb73cfa465f4cb052f3347a8651bae48140441c4fbfa753d2bd86ac41dfe8.png)\n\nA group of students is interviewed. The scatter plot above shows the correspondence between the hours they spent playing video games daily the week before the literature exam and the grade they got on the exam. Which of the following best describes the correlation between the variables in the scatter plot?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545866 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e2c56edd-8fb8-5a8e-abe8-142f14623ed7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545866 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/91/91d928d021c8715a24fdb7dc262bec53c30626d8649a2ac451c94f9c0ecc0db0.png)\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The points in the scatter plot seem to follow a straight line with a *negative* slope. So, we have a *negative* correlation.\n\n- The points lie pretty close to the corresponding trend line. So, we have a *strong* correlation.\n\nTherefore, the variables have a *strong negative* linear correlation." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545868 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186545721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545868 #edb/ref 1069 "q-134120" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545868 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545868 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/9d/9dd43d8833be95bd8b13a98b65dddd1272501599b107053296302cb7fae7e58b.png)\n\nWhich of the following is the best approximation of the correlation coefficient for the data in the scatter plot above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545868 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e6c59cf1-e1f3-5c09-adfe-bd7d9e53be52" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545868 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the linear correlation coefficient $\\rho $ satisfies the inequality\n\n$$\n-1\\le \\rho \\le 1,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\rho =1$ indicates a perfect positive correlation, and $\\rho =-1$ indicates a perfect negative correlation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our scatter plot.\n\n![](images/ae/aecc569c03ed874748993b77ed09a8a318dae23bcaea09334d98af9da36d8a87.png)\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The points in the scatter plot seem to follow a straight line with a *positive* slope. So, the correlation coefficient must be *positive*. Thus, we disregard the following values:\n\n$$\n\\rho ≈-0.75,\\,\\rho ≈-0.3,\\,\\rho ≈0\n$$\n\n- The points lie pretty close to the corresponding trend line. So, the correlation coefficient must be closer to $1.$ Thus, we disregard the following value:\n\n$$\n\\rho ≈0.25\n$$\n\nTherefore, the best approximation of the correlation coefficient must be $\\rho ≈0.85.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545874 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186545730 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545874 #edb/ref 1069 "q-133914" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545874 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545874 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/95/952ca756e237387be25ee214ba6ea2907c9af7bd6d03e9f2810648a7eb367368.png)\n\nWhich of the following best describes the correlation between the variables in the scatter plot above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545874 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f16d560c-09a5-5adf-953f-3ef2b521c424" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545874 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/d9/d91ac29530bd2408ae92cf4ea815d1a155803b8144994184cbaca09a8c0f79e4.png)\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The points in the scatter plot seem to follow a straight line with a *negative* slope. So, we have a *negative* correlation.\n\n- The points are scattered quite far from the corresponding trend line. So, we have a *weak* correlation.\n\nTherefore, the variables have a *weak negative* linear correlation." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545878 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186545706 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545878 #edb/ref 1069 "q-134091" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545878 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545878 #edb/ref 1071 "It's known that there is a strong negative linear correlation between the variables $x$ and $y.$ Which of the following scatter plots corresponds to this situation?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545878 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fdb0f84a-e85a-559c-9fae-e9b6176fb94c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545878 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine the given situation.\n\n- The *negative* linear correlation means that the data points should be scattered along a line with a *negative* slope.\n\n- Since the correlation is *strong*, most of the points should be scattered *close* to the trend line.\n\nTherefore, this situation corresponds to the following scatter plot.\n\n![](images/8c/8ca401fd830f94dae1f40d024af0362491165f91cbde4cb6fb783124e241ca4b.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
